er.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff33505b-c356-52a3-a7a0-45d1ab153c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352256} :answer-field/id #uuid "77a6ed93-55fe-5dd4-9aad-5afafd72a8fc" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8a6c00f3-7fad-59bb-b627-6aa05a1af8fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-270970" :question/problem "Let $p(x)=-30{x}^{4}-127{x}^{3}-68{x}^{2}+123x-18,$ and $p(-3)=p(-2)=0.$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $p(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $p(-3)=p(-2)=0,$ by factor theorem $(x+3)$ and $(x+2)$ are both factors of $p(x).$\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x+3)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x+3)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $-30$ | $-127$ | $-68$ | $123$ | $-18$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $90$ | $111$ | $-129$ | $18$ |\n|  | $-30$ | $-37$ | $43$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{-30{x}^{4}-127{x}^{3}-68{x}^{2}+123x-18}{x+3}=-30{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}+43x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n-30{x}^{4}-127{x}^{3}-68{x}^{2}+123x-18=(x+3)(-30{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}+43x-6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+2)$ is also a factor of $p(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(-30{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}+43x-6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $-30$ | $-37$ | $43$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $60$ | $-46$ | $6$ |\n|  | $-30$ | $23$ | $-3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{-30{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}+43x-6}{x+2}=-30{x}^{2}+23x-3,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n-30{x}^{4}-127{x}^{3}-68{x}^{2}+123x-18=(x+2)(x+3)(-30{x}^{2}+23x-3).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n8{x}^{4}+26{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-14x+3=-(x+2)(x+3)(6x-1)(5x-3).\n$$\n\nWe can now equate each factor to zero and solve for $x$ to find the four roots. In ascending order, the roots are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3\\,,\\,x=-2\\,,\\,x=\\frac{1}{6}\\,,\\,x=\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd03f31e-360e-5cc1-9118-f71f813d572e" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial With Only Two Real Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21124f9e-5634-5c50-bc08-01e332e9ad43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^2-3x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21124f9e-5634-5c50-bc08-01e332e9ad43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^2-3x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352259} :answer-field/id #uuid "67a36b24-0f0f-50e1-8231-ff254d2f0639" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa53a8b9-1e03-5ca3-b366-598fe4cf1da5" :question/math-academy-id "q-270972" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that $2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192=(x+4)(x-6)P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192.$ We know that $(x+4)$ and $(x-6)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+4)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $2$ | $-7$ | $-34$ | $56$ | $-192$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-8$ | $60$ | $-104$ | $192$ |\n|  | $2$ | $-15$ | $26$ | $-48$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192}{x+4}=2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+26x-48,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192=(x+4)(2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+26x-48).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-6)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+26x-48).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-6)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $6$ | $2$ | $-15$ | $26$ | $-48$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $12$ | $-18$ | $48$ |\n|  | $2$ | $-3$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+26x-48}{x-6}=2{x}^{2}-3x+8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192 & =(x+4)(x-6)(2{x}^{2}-3x+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $2{x}^{2}-3x+8$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac=(-3)^{2}-4(2)(8)=-55<0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $2{x}^{2}-3x+8$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192=(x+4)(x-6){{(2{x}^{2}-3x+8)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $P(x)=2{x}^{2}-3x+8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e897cc-b3fe-5d2f-8b47-3afdfe276bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "590472f9-02b3-566f-9f21-f3dd5247618f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2+x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352262} {:answer/id #uuid "812ffc62-3765-508e-93e5-6201ecaba3df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "812ffc62-3765-508e-93e5-6201ecaba3df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352263} :answer-field/id #uuid "31f36e25-b61f-5321-ad42-f00f24ae5587" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a62cf7f-46f6-57be-87c1-f3c7b68c20fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-328505" :question/problem "The polynomial \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12\n$$\n\n \ncan be expressed as\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x-3)\\cdot P(x).\n$$\n\nFind the polynomial $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12.$ We know that $(x-2)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $16$ | $-12$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $-10$ | $12$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12}{x-2}={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12=(x-2)({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-3)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $3$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6}{x-3}={x}^{2}+x-2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12=(x-2)(x-3)({x}^{2}+x-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(x)={x}^{2}+x-2=(x+2)(x-1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98c87ea4-2431-5088-8717-815efd1403ff" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "611e6ae3-e8a0-5a8b-a3e2-91b670bf956a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "611e6ae3-e8a0-5a8b-a3e2-91b670bf956a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352266} :answer-field/id #uuid "476d9f87-bf60-58d1-9c64-a6fd57a3dc0d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352265} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a13e4dc-dfd1-517c-99a4-37a016dcd16a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a13e4dc-dfd1-517c-99a4-37a016dcd16a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352268} :answer-field/id #uuid "74ac9878-47d1-56f8-affe-308452979540" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352267} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5d486e0-3b9b-5b1f-b764-a917ef050272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5d486e0-3b9b-5b1f-b764-a917ef050272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352270} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f350c97-71df-5365-8552-69cdddf7164b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352269} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aae33920-0c47-5d40-ac65-184f88efe5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aae33920-0c47-5d40-ac65-184f88efe5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352272} :answer-field/id #uuid "5822407b-3349-50ff-b1e1-2529259684f9" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efdb4184-6a9e-56dd-a9ae-479b469237ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-328522" :question/problem "Let $p(x)={x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}-9x+18,$ and $p(3)=p(-3)=0.$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $p(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $p(3)=p(-3)=0,$ by factor theorem $(x-3)$ and $(x+3)$ are both factors of $p(x).$\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x-3)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-11$ | $-9$ | $18$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $12$ | $3$ | $-18$ |\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}-9x+18}{x-3}={x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}-9x+18=(x-3)({x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+x-6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+3)$ is also a factor of $p(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+x-6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division once more.\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $1$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-3$ | $-3$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+x-6}{x+3}={x}^{2}+x-2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}-9x+18=(x-3)(x+3)({x}^{2}+x-2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}-9x+18=(x-3)(x+3)(x-1)(x+2).\n$$\n\nWe can now equate each factor to zero and solve for $x$ to find the four roots. In ascending order, the roots are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3\\,,\\,x=-2\\,,\\,x=1\\,,\\,x=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd03f31e-360e-5cc1-9118-f71f813d572e" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial With Only Two Real Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6419ccb-bf68-507d-8785-637cf251e0e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^2+x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352275} {:answer/id #uuid "65b5124c-1076-5254-b149-6384f637c7b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^2-x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65b5124c-1076-5254-b149-6384f637c7b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^2-x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352276} :answer-field/id #uuid "c605c350-1b5c-54ca-b741-b275800ccbe3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "15632de3-4f5b-5624-9dbb-26d8da6af72b" :question/math-academy-id "q-328607" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that $-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60=-(x+3)(x-4)P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60.$ We know that $(x+3)$ and $(x-4)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.  \n\nBecause $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:  \n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $-3$ | $4$ | $30$ | $-7$ | $60$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $-39$ | $27$ | $-60$ |\n|  | $-3$ | $13$ | $-9$ | $20$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,  \n\n$$\n\\frac{-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60}{x+3}=-3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}-9x+20,\n$$\n\nwhich means that  \n\n$$\n-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60=(x+3)(-3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}-9x+20).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-4)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(-3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}-9x+20).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division once more:  \n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-3$ | $13$ | $-9$ | $20$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-12$ | $4$ | $-20$ |\n|  | $-3$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,  \n\n$$\n\\frac{-3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}-9x+20}{x-4}=-3{x}^{2}+x-5,\n$$\n\nwhich means that  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60 & =(x+3)(x-4)(-3{x}^{2}+x-5) \\\\  & =-(x+3)(x-4)(3{x}^{2}-x+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $3{x}^{2}-x+5$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:  \n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac=(-1)^{2}-4(3)(5)=-59<0.\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $3{x}^{2}-x+5$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative.  \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by  \n\n$$\n-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60=-(x+3)(x-4){{(3{x}^{2}-x+5)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $P(x)=3{x}^{2}-x+5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98c87ea4-2431-5088-8717-815efd1403ff" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fd7ebdb-d3f2-5c23-82a3-7867f3e49d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fd7ebdb-d3f2-5c23-82a3-7867f3e49d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352279} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a65751a-8fe1-523f-ada6-7ecb3a34f41d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352278} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "178fab1b-c9f9-5a49-bb8e-e7eb26723836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "178fab1b-c9f9-5a49-bb8e-e7eb26723836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352281} :answer-field/id #uuid "a57cb645-f6d3-5d81-b37a-d2cecdfc3e7f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352280} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64ecfdf9-e569-53c4-aecc-7866eee3a225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64ecfdf9-e569-53c4-aecc-7866eee3a225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352283} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e9703f4-c6bd-5964-a8cb-f746286501f6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352282} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81cc1c8f-6444-5e65-ace3-67224bdb54da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81cc1c8f-6444-5e65-ace3-67224bdb54da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352285} :answer-field/id #uuid "91652596-10ab-5de2-9cd2-c83b969c802a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac2f2e0c-dde6-5502-8fcb-0850f4024a3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-337081" :question/problem "Let $p(x)=9{x}^{4}+42{x}^{3}+37{x}^{2}-28x-12,$ and $p(-2)=p(-3)=0.$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $p(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $p(-2)=p(-3)=0,$ by factor theorem $(x+2)$ and $(x+3)$ are both factors of $p(x).$\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x+2)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x+2)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $9$ | $42$ | $37$ | $-28$ | $-12$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-18$ | $-48$ | $22$ | $12$ |\n|  | $9$ | $24$ | $-11$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{4}+42{x}^{3}+37{x}^{2}-28x-12}{x+2}=9{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-11x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n9{x}^{4}+42{x}^{3}+37{x}^{2}-28x-12=(x+2)(9{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-11x-6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+3)$ is also a factor of $p(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(9{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-11x-6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $9$ | $24$ | $-11$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-27$ | $9$ | $6$ |\n|  | $9$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-11x-6}{x+3}=9{x}^{2}-3x-2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n9{x}^{4}+42{x}^{3}+37{x}^{2}-28x-12=(x+2)(x+3)(9{x}^{2}-3x-2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n9{x}^{4}+42{x}^{3}+37{x}^{2}-28x-12=(x+2)(x+3)(3x+1)(3x-2).\n$$\n\nWe can now equate each factor to zero and solve for $x$ to find the four roots. In ascending order, the roots are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3\\,,\\,x=-2\\,,\\,x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\,,\\,x=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd03f31e-360e-5cc1-9118-f71f813d572e" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial With Only Two Real Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e4cc33-c645-586c-b402-327a1164d410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+3x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352288} {:answer/id #uuid "e7db2bed-dcc9-5307-a182-44314e95eda6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-6x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352289} {:answer/id #uuid "a90203e3-f8a8-52fc-8abd-fdc81d521c17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352290} {:answer/id #uuid "0ac08a08-a9f9-5a88-a56d-cead471dc033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352291} {:answer/id #uuid "61add5c2-d6e6-583d-810d-a1eb6cf4cdfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-4x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ac08a08-a9f9-5a88-a56d-cead471dc033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352291} :answer-field/id #uuid "47685423-549b-5018-8abb-c7a6fd590652" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2815507f-984e-5b26-8c90-fe207af0e262" :question/math-academy-id "q-75800" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that \n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-2=(x+1)(x-2)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-2.$ We know that $(x-2)$ and $(x+1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n|  | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-2}{x-2}=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-2=(x-2)(2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+1).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+1}{x+1}=2{x}^{2}-x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-2=(x-2)(x+1)(2{x}^{2}-x+1).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $2{x}^{2}-x+1$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac=(-1)^{2}-4(2)(1)=-7<0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $2{x}^{2}-x+1$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-2=(x-2)(x+1){{(2{x}^{2}-x+1)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $P(x)=2{x}^{2}-x+1.$"}]}
